WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:09.647
IAN SHELBACK: Hello and welcome to the Little Fox Animal Report.

2
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:11.208
IAN SHELBACK: I am your host, Ian Shelback.

3
00:00:11.228 --> 00:00:14.450
IAN SHELBACK: This week, I am in the frozen Arctic.

4
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.613
IAN SHELBACK: I am interviewing a very handsome polar bear named Chilly.

5
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:21.155
IAN SHELBACK: Chilly has kindly agreed not to eat me.

6
00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:22.796
IAN SHELBACK: Good afternoon, Chilly.

7
00:00:22.977 --> 00:00:24.277
You look very handsome today.

8
00:00:24.298 --> 00:00:25.979
CHILLY: Hello, Ian.

9
00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:27.320
It’s nice to see you.

10
00:00:27.808 --> 00:00:32.251
IAN: People want to know what life is like for a polar bear, [n3@living so far north in

11
00:00:32.291 --> 00:00:33.331
this frozen country.

12
00:00:33.351 --> 00:00:38.094
CHILLY: Well, first of all, it is not a life for the weak.

13
00:00:38.194 --> 00:00:40.756
CHILLY: Many animals would not like it here.

14
00:00:41.096 --> 00:00:41.656
It's very cold.

15
00:00:41.716 --> 00:00:46.219
CHILLY: The Arctic is only for those of us who are hardy and strong.

16
00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:51.102
CHILLY: I am one of the strongest creatures in the world, [n3@and I am not afraid of anything

17
00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:51.822
or anyone.

18
00:00:51.842 --> 00:00:54.704
IAN: You do look very strong and large.

19
00:00:55.412 --> 00:00:58.013
IAN: If you don't mind me asking, how much do you weigh?

20
00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:03.896
CHILLY: Well, I weigh 720 kilograms, which is big even for a polar bear.

21
00:01:03.916 --> 00:01:10.218
CHILLY: I am 160 centimeters tall, and I am 250 centimeters [n3@from head to tail.

22
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:11.959
I'm not a cub!

23
00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:13.900
IAN: No, that's for sure.

24
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.321
You must be one of the [n3@biggest and strongest bears around.

25
00:01:17.341 --> 00:01:21.623
IAN: Tell me, Chilly, don't your feet get cold standing on the snow all day?

26
00:01:21.643 --> 00:01:23.944
CHILLY: No, not at all.

27
00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:28.373
CHILLY: My fur covers the bottom of my feet and keeps me very warm.

28
00:01:28.393 --> 00:01:32.934
CHILLY: I certainly don't need silly-looking boots like you are wearing.

29
00:01:32.954 --> 00:01:34.975
CHILLY: Look around, Ian.

30
00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:37.376
How many polar bears can you see?

31
00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:41.897
IAN: Hmm, I don't see any polar bears, Chilly.

32
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:45.298
CHILLY: Right now there are ten polar bears watching you.

33
00:01:45.318 --> 00:01:48.879
CHILLY: But you cannot see them because they are camouflaged.

34
00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:50.379
IAN: Really?

35
00:01:50.699 --> 00:01:52.360
That is very good camouflage.

36
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.601
CHILLY: It sure is.

37
00:01:54.921 --> 00:01:57.003
Hey, guys, say hello to Ian.

38
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:58.624
BEARS: Hello, Ian.

39
00:01:59.024 --> 00:02:00.145
How are you today?

40
00:02:00.165 --> 00:02:01.827
IAN: Fine, thank you.

41
00:02:02.427 --> 00:02:08.292
CHILLY: Camouflage is very important for us because it helps [n3@us stay undetected when we are hunting.

42
00:02:08.312 --> 00:02:11.134
IAN: And what exactly do you hunt?

43
00:02:11.154 --> 00:02:13.516
CHILLY: Our favorite prey is seal.

44
00:02:13.536 --> 00:02:18.300
CHILLY: But seals are not easy to catch, so we have to be very good hunters.

45
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.841
IAN: How do you catch them?

46
00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:22.944
CHILLY: Well, you have to be very patient.

47
00:02:23.532 --> 00:02:27.775
CHILLY: First, I look for a hole in the ice where [n3@the seals like to come to rest.

48
00:02:28.476 --> 00:02:32.438
CHILLY: Then I hide myself beside the hole and wait until a seal appears.

49
00:02:32.458 --> 00:02:37.002
CHILLY: When a seal climbs out of the hole, I grab it and eat it.

50
00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:40.124
IAN: What do you do if the seal escapes?

51
00:02:40.804 --> 00:02:41.365
CHILLY: Ha-ha!

52
00:02:41.705 --> 00:02:44.007
The seal almost never gets away from me.

53
00:02:44.027 --> 00:02:47.189
CHILLY: But if it does, I chase it underwater.

54
00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:51.452
IAN: If you can chase a seal underwater, you must be a pretty good swimmer.

55
00:02:52.748 --> 00:02:54.870
CHILLY: Polar bears are exceptional swimmers.

56
00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.393
CHILLY: Many people call us the sea bear because [n3@we can swim so far and fast.

57
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:04.857
CHILLY: Sometimes I spend almost all day swimming in the freezing waters.

58
00:03:04.877 --> 00:03:07.118
IAN: Many animals hibernate.

59
00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:09.680
You know, they go to sleep during the winter.

60
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.601
IAN: What about polar bears?

61
00:03:12.302 --> 00:03:13.022
Do you hibernate?

62
00:03:13.042 --> 00:03:15.164
CHILLY: Are you kidding?

63
00:03:15.864 --> 00:03:17.866
No, polar bears do not hibernate.

64
00:03:17.886 --> 00:03:20.988
CHILLY: We are too busy to waste time sleeping.

65
00:03:21.892 --> 00:03:23.413
CHILLY: We are very hard workers.

66
00:03:24.094 --> 00:03:26.156
But we only eat in the spring and fall.

67
00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:30.119
CHILLY: During the rest of the year, there is no food for us to eat.

68
00:03:30.139 --> 00:03:31.300
IAN: Really?

69
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.601
How do you survive?

70
00:03:32.621 --> 00:03:38.826
CHILLY: In the spring, when the ice breaks up, we can catch plenty of [n3@seals because there are

71
00:03:38.906 --> 00:03:40.087
many holes in the ice.

72
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:46.012
CHILLY: However, in the summer, when all of the ice is gone, [n3@it is too difficult to catch

73
00:03:46.032 --> 00:03:50.996
seals. CHILLY: In the fall, we can catch seals when the ice starts to freeze again.

74
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.622
CHILLY: But in the winter, the ice is too thick for us to get the seals.

75
00:03:55.642 --> 00:04:00.645
CHILLY: We are able to eat an enormous amount of food at [n3@one time while the food is

76
00:04:00.705 --> 00:04:06.368
available, CHILLY: and then store our food as fat for many months for [n3@when fresh prey is not

77
00:04:06.408 --> 00:04:09.669
available. IAN: That is very interesting.

78
00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:11.210
What about sports?

79
00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.052
IAN: What do polar bears like to do for fun?

80
00:04:14.072 --> 00:04:17.033
CHILLY: Our favorite sport is wrestling.

81
00:04:17.914 --> 00:04:19.735
I happen to be a champion wrestler.

82
00:04:19.755 --> 00:04:21.676
IAN: I'm sure you are.

83
00:04:22.548 --> 00:04:23.468
How do you usually play?

84
00:04:23.528 --> 00:04:25.569
CHILLY: Let's watch!

85
00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:28.530
IAN: How do you choose a winner?

86
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:34.751
CHILLY: When one bear gets too tired or sore, he lies down on [n3@the ground, and the other

87
00:04:34.831 --> 00:04:35.752
bear is the winner.

88
00:04:35.772 --> 00:04:37.872
CHILLY: I am always the winner.

89
00:04:37.892 --> 00:04:40.473
IAN: I am sure you are, Chilly.

90
00:04:41.373 --> 00:04:42.333
What about family?

91
00:04:43.174 --> 00:04:44.274
Do you have any children?

92
00:04:44.294 --> 00:04:46.614
CHILLY: I have a lot of children.

93
00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:50.355
CHILLY: In fact, my wife will be having three new cubs soon.

94
00:04:50.375 --> 00:04:52.136
IAN: Where is she now?

95
00:04:53.148 --> 00:04:54.069
CHILLY: She is in her den.

96
00:04:54.089 --> 00:04:59.271
CHILLY: When a polar bear is going to have cubs, she [n3@digs a large hole in the snow.

97
00:04:59.291 --> 00:05:03.614
CHILLY: Just before the cubs come, she goes and lives in the den.

98
00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:06.295
IAN: Do you live in the den with her?

99
00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:07.956
CHILLY: No way!

100
00:05:08.356 --> 00:05:10.297
I stay out in the cold all the time.

101
00:05:10.317 --> 00:05:13.959
CHILLY: Remember, polar bears are very tough animals.

102
00:05:13.999 --> 00:05:20.543
CHILLY: By the way, Ian, there is a polar bear over there that seems [n3@very hungry, and he

103
00:05:20.623 --> 00:05:22.064
is eyeing you hungrily.

104
00:05:22.764 --> 00:05:24.365
CHILLY: Maybe you should be going now.

105
00:05:24.386 --> 00:05:26.548
IAN: That's a good idea.

106
00:05:27.268 --> 00:05:29.190
Thank you very much for the interview, Chilly.

107
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.572
IAN: It was a real pleasure to meet you.

108
00:05:31.592 --> 00:05:36.136
CHILLY: I'm sure it was an honor for you to meet a great [n3@polar bear like me.

109
00:05:36.817 --> 00:05:37.438
Good-bye, Ian.

110
00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:39.479
IAN: Let's get out of here!
